// Deep-dive layer: John and Acts. Format as in dd_a.js.
export default {
  42: [
    [2, `Water into wine, and a whip of cords`,
      `John 2 begins with the first of his “signs”: at a wedding in Cana the wine runs out, and Jesus’ mother says, “They have no wine.” He answers, “My hour has not yet come” (2:4), but turns about 120 to 180 gallons of water in six stone purification jars into wine, which the master of the feast calls the best, kept until last (2:10). John calls it the first sign that “manifested his glory” (2:11). Then Jesus goes to Jerusalem for Passover, makes a whip of cords, drives out animals and overturns the moneychangers’ tables (2:13-16). In John this happens at the beginning of the ministry, not the end. Asked for a sign, he says, “Destroy this temple, and in three days I will raise it up,” and John explains that he spoke about “the temple of his body” (2:19-21). The jars for ritual washing become vessels of celebration.`,
      [[`Isaiah 25:6`, `A feast of well-aged wine in the end times.`], [`Malachi 3:1`, `“The Lord whom you seek will suddenly come to his temple.”`]],
      [`The best wine is saved for last. What does that say about how John pictures Jesus’ work compared with what came before?`, `John places the temple scene first, Mark and the others last. What might each placement emphasise?`],
      [`choose`, `What is the main point of Cana?`, `Jesus replaces ritual purification with the abundance of the new age.`, `Jesus’ glory is revealed in an ordinary celebration.`]],
    [4, `A woman at a well`,
      `John 4 is the longest private conversation in the Gospels. Jesus, tired, sits by Jacob’s well in Samaria at noon and asks a woman for a drink, which breaks conventions of ethnicity and gender (4:9). He offers “living water” (4:10). When he asks her to call her husband, she says she has none, and he says she has had five husbands and the present man is not her husband (4:16-18). She changes the subject to worship: “Our fathers worshipped on this mountain, but you say that in Jerusalem is the place” (4:20). He answers, “The hour is coming, and is now here, when the true worshippers will worship the Father in spirit and in truth” (4:23). She says, “I know that Messiah is coming,” and he says, “I who speak to you am he” (4:25-26). She leaves her jar and tells the town, and many Samaritans believe.`,
      [[`Genesis 29:1-12`, `Betrothal scenes at wells.`], [`Jeremiah 2:13`, `“Fountain of living waters.”`]],
      [`She became the first evangelist in John, with a past that others would hide. What does that say about whom God uses?`, `Worship “in spirit and in truth.” What does that remove from worship, and what does it add?`],
      [`choose`, `How do you read the five husbands?`, `As a literal history that Jesus reveals.`, `As partly symbolic of Samaria’s five foreign gods.`]],
    [5, `A pool, a Sabbath, and a defence`,
      `At the pool of Bethesda, a man has been ill for thirty-eight years. Jesus asks, “Do you want to be healed?” and the man explains that no one will help him into the water. Jesus says, “Get up, take up your bed, and walk” (5:8). It is the Sabbath, and the authorities object. Jesus defends himself: “My Father is working until now, and I am working” (5:17), and John says they sought to kill him because he “was even calling God his own Father, making himself equal with God” (5:18). A long discourse follows: the Son does what he sees the Father do (5:19), gives life and judges (5:21-22), and “the hour is coming when the dead will hear the voice of the Son of God, and those who hear will live” (5:25). He cites four witnesses: John, his works, the Father and the scriptures (5:31-47). “It is they that bear witness about me” (5:39).`,
      [[`Exodus 20:8-11`, `The Sabbath command.`], [`Deuteronomy 18:15-19`, `“A prophet like me,” which Jesus refers to in 5:46.`]],
      [`Jesus asks “Do you want to be healed?” to a man ill for thirty-eight years. Why ask?`, `“You search the scriptures… yet you refuse to come to me.” How does that warn those who know the text?`],
      [`choose`, `What is the main claim in 5:17?`, `That God’s work does not stop on the Sabbath, and neither does the Son’s.`, `That Jesus shares the Father’s authority.`]],
    [6, `Bread from heaven, and a hard saying`,
      `John 6 begins with the feeding of the five thousand, followed by Jesus walking on the sea. The next day the crowd seeks him, and he says, “You are seeking me, not because you saw signs, but because you ate your fill of the loaves” (6:26). “I am the bread of life; whoever comes to me shall not hunger” (6:35). When he adds, “unless you eat the flesh of the Son of Man and drink his blood, you have no life in you” (6:53), many disciples say, “This is a hard saying; who can listen to it?” (6:60) and “many of his disciples turned back and no longer walked with him” (6:66). Jesus asks the twelve if they also want to go, and Peter answers, “Lord, to whom shall we go? You have the words of eternal life” (6:68). The discourse is read in some traditions as eucharistic, in others as symbolic of faith.`,
      [[`Exodus 16:4-15`, `Manna in the wilderness.`], [`Deuteronomy 8:3`, `“Man does not live by bread alone.”`]],
      [`Many leave when the teaching gets hard. What teaching tests you?`, `Peter does not say he understands, but says there is nowhere else to go. How is that a kind of faith?`],
      [`choose`, `How do you read the flesh and blood teaching?`, `As a reference to the Lord’s Supper.`, `As a figure for receiving Jesus himself through faith.`]],
    [7, `Living water at the feast of Booths`,
      `John 7 takes place at the Feast of Booths (Tabernacles), a week of celebration when water was drawn from the pool of Siloam and poured out at the altar. His brothers urge him to go to Jerusalem and show himself, “for not even his brothers believed in him” (7:5). He goes privately, then teaches midway through the feast and says, “If anyone thirsts, let him come to me and drink. Whoever believes in me, as the Scripture has said, ‘Out of his heart will flow rivers of living water’” (7:37-38). John comments that he meant the Spirit, not yet given. The crowd is divided: “Has the Christ come from Galilee?” (7:41). The temple guards report, “No one ever spoke like this man!” (7:46). Nicodemus asks if the law judges a man without hearing him, and is mocked (7:50-52). The story of the woman caught in adultery (7:53-8:11) is absent from the earliest manuscripts.`,
      [[`Isaiah 55:1`, `“Everyone who thirsts, come to the waters.”`], [`Zechariah 14:8`, `Living waters flow out from Jerusalem.`]],
      [`Jesus speaks on the last day of the feast, when water is poured out. How does the setting sharpen the claim?`, `The guards return without arresting him. What happens when you really listen?`],
      [`choose`, `What is the “living water”?`, `The Holy Spirit given after Jesus’ glorification.`, `Jesus’ teaching and life-giving presence.`]],
    [8, `Light of the world, and before Abraham was`,
      `After the Feast of Booths, in which great lamps were lit in the temple courts, Jesus says, “I am the light of the world. Whoever follows me will not walk in darkness, but will have the light of life” (8:12). A long argument follows. The Pharisees say his testimony is not valid on his own, and he says it is valid because he knows where he came from. “If you abide in my word, you are truly my disciples, and you will know the truth, and the truth will set you free” (8:31-32). His hearers say they have never been slaves, and he says, “Everyone who commits sin is a slave to sin” (8:34). The exchange escalates, and he says, “Your father Abraham rejoiced that he would see my day.” They ask, “You are not yet fifty years old, and have you seen Abraham?” He answers, “Truly, truly, I say to you, before Abraham was, I am” (8:56-58), and they pick up stones.`,
      [[`Exodus 3:14`, `“I AM WHO I AM.”`], [`Isaiah 43:10-11`, `“That you may know… that I am he.”`]],
      [`“The truth will set you free.” How often is this quoted apart from the condition “if you abide in my word”?`, `Why do they pick up stones at “I am”?`],
      [`choose`, `What does “before Abraham was, I am” claim?`, `That Jesus shares the divine name and pre-exists Abraham.`, `That Jesus’ identity transcends time as the agent of God’s purposes.`]],
    [10, `The good shepherd, and the Feast of Dedication`,
      `John 10 opens with the sheepfold: the shepherd enters by the gate, the sheep hear his voice and he calls them by name. “I am the door of the sheep” (10:7), and “I am the good shepherd. The good shepherd lays down his life for the sheep” (10:11). The hired hand flees when the wolf comes. “I have other sheep that are not of this fold. I must bring them also… So there will be one flock, one shepherd” (10:16). At the Feast of Dedication (Hanukkah), in winter, in Solomon’s portico, the Jews ask, “If you are the Christ, tell us plainly.” He answers that his works testify, “My sheep hear my voice, and I know them, and they follow me” (10:27), and “I and the Father are one” (10:30). They take up stones again, and he quotes Psalm 82:6, “I said, you are gods,” to defend the claim (10:34-36).`,
      [[`Ezekiel 34:11-16`, `God as the shepherd who seeks the lost.`], [`Psalm 82:6`, `“You are gods,” the text Jesus quotes.`]],
      [`The good shepherd lays down his life voluntarily. What makes that different from a hero’s death?`, `He quotes Psalm 82 to defend his claim. What does his use of an obscure verse show about his argument?`],
      [`choose`, `How do you read “other sheep”?`, `A reference to Gentiles who will join the one flock.`, `A reference to scattered Jews.`]],
    [12, `Mary’s perfume, a donkey, and a seed that dies`,
      `Six days before Passover, Mary of Bethany anoints Jesus’ feet with a pound of costly nard and wipes them with her hair, and the house is filled with the fragrance (12:1-3). Judas objects, and John adds that he was a thief who held the money bag (12:6). Jesus enters Jerusalem on a donkey to crowds waving palm branches and shouting, “Hosanna! Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord” (12:13). Some Greeks ask to see Jesus, and he responds, “The hour has come for the Son of Man to be glorified. Truly, truly, I say to you, unless a grain of wheat falls into the earth and dies, it remains alone; but if it dies, it bears much fruit” (12:23-24). “And I, when I am lifted up from the earth, will draw all people to myself” (12:32). John closes with the verdict of unbelief, quoting Isaiah 53:1 and 6:10 (12:37-41).`,
      [[`Zechariah 9:9`, `The king coming on a donkey.`], [`Isaiah 53:1`, `“Who has believed what he has heard from us?”`]],
      [`Mary pours out something worth about a year’s wages. What would count as a costly gift for you?`, `The Greeks’ request marks the hour. Why would the arrival of outsiders trigger the sense that the time has come?`],
      [`choose`, `What does the grain of wheat teach?`, `Fruitfulness comes through death.`, `Jesus’ death will be the source of a worldwide community.`]],
    [13, `A towel, and a new commandment`,
      `John 13 starts the Farewell Discourse. Before the Passover meal Jesus, “knowing that the Father had given all things into his hands,” takes off his outer garment, wraps a towel around himself, and washes the disciples’ feet, the job of a lowly servant (13:3-5). Peter objects, “You shall never wash my feet,” and then asks for his whole body to be washed (13:8-9). Jesus says, “If I then, your Lord and Teacher, have washed your feet, you also ought to wash one another’s feet” (13:14). He identifies his betrayer, and Judas goes out: “And it was night” (13:30). Then: “A new commandment I give to you, that you love one another: just as I have loved you, you also are to love one another. By this all people will know that you are my disciples” (13:34-35). Peter swears he will lay down his life, and Jesus predicts his denial.`,
      [[`Luke 22:24-27`, `“I am among you as the one who serves.”`], [`Leviticus 19:18`, `The older command to love one’s neighbour.`]],
      [`Jesus washes the feet of the one who will betray him. What does that say about the limits of service?`, `The command is called new though Leviticus already had love of neighbour. What is new in “as I have loved you”?`],
      [`rate`, `How readily could you do a menial task for someone you know has wronged you?`, `Reluctantly`, `Willingly`]],
    [14, `Many rooms, and the way`,
      `John 14 opens with comfort: “Let not your hearts be troubled. Believe in God; believe also in me. In my Father’s house are many rooms… I go to prepare a place for you” (14:1-2). Thomas says, “We do not know where you are going. How can we know the way?” Jesus answers, “I am the way, and the truth, and the life. No one comes to the Father except through me” (14:6). Philip asks, “Show us the Father,” and Jesus replies, “Whoever has seen me has seen the Father” (14:9). He promises “greater works” and the Spirit as “another Helper,” the *paraklētos*, who will be with them forever (14:12-17). “Peace I leave with you; my peace I give to you. Not as the world gives do I give to you” (14:27). The chapter ends with “Rise, let us go from here,” and the discourse continues in chapters 15-17.`,
      [[`Psalm 23:6`, `Dwelling in the house of the LORD.`], [`Romans 8:26-27`, `The Spirit helps in weakness.`]],
      [`Thomas’s honest question is answered with “I am the way.” What does it mean that the way is a person rather than a map?`, `“Another Helper.” What does “another” imply about Jesus’ own role?`],
      [`choose`, `How do you read 14:6?`, `As a claim that salvation is through Jesus alone.`, `As a statement of who Jesus is, to be read in its context of comfort to disciples.`]],
    [15, `The vine, and abiding`,
      `John 15 uses the image of the vine, an Old Testament symbol of Israel (Isaiah 5; Psalm 80). “I am the true vine, and my Father is the vinedresser” (15:1). Branches that do not bear fruit are taken away, and those that bear fruit are pruned to bear more (15:2). The key verb is “abide” (*menō*), used about eleven times in the first ten verses: “Abide in me, and I in you. As the branch cannot bear fruit by itself, unless it abides in the vine, neither can you, unless you abide in me” (15:4). “Apart from me you can do nothing” (15:5). Then love: “Greater love has no one than this, that someone lay down his life for his friends” (15:13). “You did not choose me, but I chose you and appointed you that you should go and bear fruit” (15:16). The chapter ends with the world’s hatred and the testimony of the Spirit.`,
      [[`Isaiah 5:1-7`, `Israel as the vineyard that failed.`], [`Psalm 80:8-19`, `The vine brought from Egypt.`]],
      [`Pruning is part of the vinedresser’s care. How do you interpret a loss in your life as pruning, and when is that unhelpful?`, `“Abide” is a single instruction repeated. What would abiding look like in a busy week?`],
      [`choose`, `What does fruit stand for?`, `Love and obedience.`, `New disciples and the mission.`]],
    [16, `Sorrow into joy`,
      `John 16 continues the Farewell Discourse with a warning: “They will put you out of the synagogues. Indeed, the hour is coming when whoever kills you will think he is offering service to God” (16:2). Yet it is to the disciples’ advantage that he goes away, for otherwise the Helper will not come (16:7). The Spirit will “convict the world concerning sin and righteousness and judgement” (16:8) and “guide you into all the truth” (16:13). Jesus compares the disciples’ coming grief to a woman in labour: “When she has given birth to the child, she no longer remembers the anguish, for joy that a human being has been born into the world” (16:21). “Ask, and you will receive, that your joy may be full” (16:24). He concludes: “In the world you will have tribulation. But take heart; I have overcome the world” (16:33).`,
      [[`Isaiah 66:7-14`, `Labour and rejoicing in Zion.`], [`Acts 2:1-4`, `The Spirit arrives.`]],
      [`“It is to your advantage that I go away.” How could an absence be a gain?`, `Jesus promises tribulation and peace in the same breath. How do you hold both?`],
      [`choose`, `What is the main purpose of the discourse?`, `To prepare the disciples for his departure and their persecution.`, `To teach about the Spirit’s role after the resurrection.`]],
    [17, `The prayer of the Son`,
      `John 17 is Jesus’ long prayer, often called the High Priestly Prayer. He begins, “Father, the hour has come; glorify your Son” (17:1), and defines eternal life: “that they know you the only true God, and Jesus Christ whom you have sent” (17:3). He prays for the disciples: “I do not ask that you take them out of the world, but that you keep them from the evil one” (17:15). “Sanctify them in the truth; your word is truth” (17:17). Then he prays for later believers: “that they may all be one, just as you, Father, are in me, and I in you, that they also may be in us, so that the world may believe that you have sent me” (17:21). The unity is not an administrative merger but a participation in the life of the Father and the Son. The prayer ends with love: “that the love with which you have loved me may be in them, and I in them” (17:26).`,
      [[`Exodus 28:36-38`, `The high priest bears the people before God.`], [`Ephesians 4:1-6`, `One body, one Spirit.`]],
      [`Jesus prays for people who are not yet born. What does it feel like to be the subject of that prayer?`, `Unity here is meant to convince the world. What do divisions among believers communicate?`],
      [`rewrite`, `Write a short prayer for a group you belong to, modelled on John 17:21.`]],
    [19, `“It is finished”`,
      `John 19 narrates the passion with royal irony. Pilate presents a scourged Jesus in a purple robe and crown of thorns: “Behold the man!” (19:5). “Behold your king!” (19:14). When the chief priests say, “We have no king but Caesar” (19:15), they give up a central conviction of Israel. Pilate has a sign written in Hebrew, Latin and Greek: “Jesus of Nazareth, the King of the Jews” (19:19-20). Soldiers divide his garments and cast lots for the seamless tunic (Psalm 22:18). From the cross, Jesus entrusts his mother to the beloved disciple (19:26-27). He says, “I thirst,” and then, “It is finished,” bows his head and gives up his spirit (19:28-30). It is Passover preparation day, and no bone of his is broken (19:36, echoing Exodus 12:46). Joseph of Arimathea and Nicodemus bury him with about a hundred pounds of spices.`,
      [[`Exodus 12:46`, `The Passover lamb’s bones are not broken.`], [`Psalm 22:18`, `“They divide my garments among them.”`]],
      [`“We have no king but Caesar,” the priests say at Passover. What is the cost of that choice?`, `“It is finished” is one word in Greek, *tetelestai*. What might have been finished?`],
      [`choose`, `How is the cross presented in John?`, `As the enthronement of the true king.`, `As the final Passover sacrifice.`]],
    [21, `Breakfast on the beach, and “Do you love me?”`,
      `John 21 is an epilogue. Seven disciples fish all night and catch nothing until a man on the shore tells them to cast on the right side, and the net holds 153 large fish (21:11). “It is the Lord!” says the beloved disciple, and Peter jumps into the water. On the shore they find a charcoal fire, with fish and bread, and Jesus says, “Come and have breakfast” (21:12). The charcoal fire echoes the one where Peter denied him (18:18). Jesus asks Peter three times, “Do you love me?” and each time commissions him: “Feed my lambs… Tend my sheep… Feed my sheep” (21:15-17). Peter is “grieved” at the third question. Jesus foretells how Peter will die (21:18-19) and says, “Follow me.” When Peter asks about the beloved disciple, Jesus answers, “What is that to you? You follow me” (21:22). The number 153 has produced many symbolic readings; none is certain.`,
      [[`John 18:15-27`, `Peter’s denial beside another charcoal fire.`], [`Ezekiel 34:11-16`, `God’s own shepherding, now delegated.`]],
      [`Three denials, three questions, three commissions. How does the structure repair the failure?`, `“What is that to you? You follow me.” When do you compare your calling with another’s?`],
      [`choose`, `What is the aim of the threefold question?`, `To restore Peter and recommission him.`, `To bring Peter to name what he had avoided.`]]
  ],
  43: [
    [1, `Waiting, and the Spirit’s promise`,
      `Acts begins with a preface to “Theophilus” that resumes Luke’s Gospel. Over forty days the risen Jesus appears and speaks about the kingdom, then tells the apostles to wait in Jerusalem for “the promise of the Father” (1:4). They ask, “Lord, will you at this time restore the kingdom to Israel?” (1:6), and he declines to give dates and instead gives a map: “You will receive power when the Holy Spirit has come upon you, and you will be my witnesses in Jerusalem and in all Judea and Samaria, and to the end of the earth” (1:8). He ascends, and two men in white say, “Why do you stand looking into heaven?” (1:11). The group of about 120 gathers and prays with Mary and his brothers. Peter proposes to replace Judas, and Matthias is chosen by lot (1:15-26). Verse 8 is often treated as an outline of the book.`,
      [[`Luke 24:44-53`, `The ascension at the end of Luke.`], [`Psalm 69:25`, `A verse Peter applies to Judas (Acts 1:20).`]],
      [`The disciples ask about Israel’s kingdom, and Jesus gives a mission. Where do your questions about timing replace the task?`, `Why would the replacement of Judas matter to the early community?`],
      [`choose`, `What does Acts 1:8 provide?`, `A geographical outline for the book.`, `A programme of witness empowered by the Spirit.`]],
    [3, `A lame man at the Beautiful Gate`,
      `In Acts 3, Peter and John go to the temple at the hour of prayer. A man lame from birth is placed daily at the Beautiful Gate to ask for money. Peter says, “I have no silver and gold, but what I do have I give to you. In the name of Jesus Christ of Nazareth, rise up and walk!” (3:6). The man enters the temple “walking and leaping and praising God.” The crowd gathers at Solomon’s portico, and Peter says, “Why do you wonder at this, or why do you stare at us, as though by our own power or piety we had made him walk?” (3:12). He calls Jesus “the Holy and Righteous One” and says, “You killed the Author of life, whom God raised from the dead” (3:14-15), yet “I know that you acted in ignorance” (3:17). He calls for repentance, “that times of refreshing may come” (3:19), and points to Moses’ promise of a prophet like him.`,
      [[`Isaiah 35:6`, `“The lame man shall leap like a deer.”`], [`Deuteronomy 18:15`, `The prophet like Moses.`]],
      [`Peter says, “What I have I give you.” What do you have that is worth more than money?`, `Peter’s speech accuses and offers hope in the same breath. How does he balance them?`],
      [`choose`, `What does the healing signal?`, `That the age of restoration promised by the prophets has begun.`, `That the apostles continue Jesus’ work in his name.`]],
    [4, `Boldness before the council`,
      `Acts 4 opens with the arrest of Peter and John by the priests and Sadducees, who are “greatly annoyed because they were teaching the people and proclaiming in Jesus the resurrection of the dead” (4:2). Before the council, filled with the Spirit, Peter says, “There is salvation in no one else, for there is no other name under heaven given among men by which we must be saved” (4:12). The council “saw the boldness of Peter and John, and perceived that they were uneducated, common men,” and “recognised that they had been with Jesus” (4:13). Told to stop, they answer, “Whether it is right in the sight of God to listen to you rather than to God, you must judge” (4:19). The community prays using Psalm 2 and the place shakes. The chapter describes believers holding everything in common and Barnabas selling a field and bringing the money to the apostles (4:32-37).`,
      [[`Psalm 118:22`, `The rejected stone, cited in Acts 4:11.`], [`Psalm 2:1-2`, `The kings of the earth conspire, used in the prayer.`]],
      [`The council recognises that they “had been with Jesus.” What does time with Jesus produce that education does not?`, `The community’s response to threats is prayer for boldness, not safety. What do you pray for under pressure?`],
      [`choose`, `What is the main thrust of Acts 4:12?`, `An exclusive claim about Jesus as the only saviour.`, `A defiant statement of the apostles’ message in a hostile court.`]],
    [5, `Ananias and Sapphira, and a rescue from prison`,
      `Acts 5 begins with a couple who sell property and bring part of the money while pretending it is the whole. Peter says, “Why has Satan filled your heart to lie to the Holy Spirit?… You have not lied to man but to God” (5:3-4). Ananias falls dead, and Sapphira, repeating the lie, dies too. “And great fear came upon the whole church” (5:11). The sin was deception, not the amount, since the property was theirs to give or keep (5:4). The apostles do many signs, and the high priest imprisons them, but an angel opens the doors at night. Brought before the council again, Peter says, “We must obey God rather than men” (5:29). Gamaliel, a respected Pharisee, advises restraint: “If this plan or this undertaking is from man, it will fail; but if it is from God, you will not be able to overthrow them” (5:38-39). The apostles are flogged and leave rejoicing (5:41).`,
      [[`Joshua 7:1-26`, `Achan’s hidden theft, a parallel of sin in a new community.`], [`Acts 4:36-37`, `Barnabas’s sincere gift, for contrast.`]],
      [`The story is about deception in the community, not wealth. How does a lie about generosity harm a group?`, `Gamaliel’s test is “if it is from God, you will not be able to overthrow it.” What are its strengths and limits?`],
      [`choose`, `How do you read the deaths?`, `As a grave warning at the founding of the church, like Nadab and Abihu.`, `As a narrative that stresses the seriousness of lying to the Spirit.`]],
    [6, `Seven chosen, and Stephen arrested`,
      `Acts 6 shows the first internal conflict: “The Hellenists murmured against the Hebrews because their widows were being neglected in the daily distribution” (6:1). Hellenists were Greek-speaking Jewish believers. The twelve call the community together and have them choose seven “of good repute, full of the Spirit and of wisdom” (6:3). All seven have Greek names, which suggests they represented the Hellenist group. The apostles commit to “prayer and the ministry of the word.” “And the word of God continued to increase” (6:7). Stephen, “full of grace and power,” does wonders, and is accused of speaking against the temple and the law. “They saw that his face was like the face of an angel” (6:15). The trial scene sets up chapter 7, a long speech reviewing Israel’s history. The seven are often called deacons, though Acts does not use that word for them.`,
      [[`Numbers 11:16-17`, `Moses chooses seventy elders to share the load.`], [`Exodus 18:13-26`, `Jethro advises Moses to delegate.`]],
      [`The solution to a dispute about widows is to appoint people from the neglected group. What does that teach about handling grievances?`, `The apostles keep their focus on prayer and word. What is your equivalent?`],
      [`choose`, `What does the choice of seven show?`, `A community responsive to the neglected, giving leadership to the group in question.`, `A division of labour for ministry and administration.`]],
    [8, `Samaria, and an Ethiopian official`,
      `After Stephen’s death, persecution scatters believers, and Philip goes to Samaria and proclaims Christ; people are healed and “there was much joy in that city” (8:8). Simon the magician believes, but tries to buy the apostles’ power, and Peter says, “Your silver perish with you” (8:20). An angel directs Philip to a desert road, where he meets an Ethiopian eunuch, the treasurer of the Candace (a title for the queen mother of Meroë), reading Isaiah 53 in his chariot. “Do you understand what you are reading?” “How can I, unless someone guides me?” (8:30-31). Philip begins with that passage and tells him the good news of Jesus. “See, here is water! What prevents me from being baptised?” (8:36). After baptism, the Spirit carries Philip away, and the official “went on his way rejoicing” (8:39). Here the gospel crosses boundaries of ethnicity and body.`,
      [[`Isaiah 53:7-8`, `The passage the official reads.`], [`Isaiah 56:3-5`, `The promise to eunuchs who hold to the covenant.`]],
      [`The official asks, “What prevents me?” What would you have said as a person standing on that road?`, `Isaiah 56 promises a place to eunuchs. How does this scene fulfil it?`],
      [`choose`, `What is the importance of the Ethiopian in Acts?`, `An early step of the gospel toward “the end of the earth.”`, `A signal that outsiders by body or origin are welcomed.`]],
    [9, `A persecutor on the Damascus road`,
      `Acts 9 introduces Saul, “still breathing threats and murder against the disciples,” who goes to Damascus with letters from the high priest (9:1-2). A light from heaven flashes, and a voice says, “Saul, Saul, why are you persecuting me?” (9:4). “Who are you, Lord?” “I am Jesus, whom you are persecuting” (9:5). Saul is blind for three days. A disciple named Ananias is told in a vision to go to him, and objects, “Lord, I have heard from many about this man, how much evil he has done to your saints” (9:13). The Lord answers, “He is a chosen instrument of mine to carry my name before the Gentiles and kings and the children of Israel. For I will show him how much he must suffer for the sake of my name” (9:15-16). Ananias lays hands on him, and something like scales fall from his eyes. He is baptised and begins to preach, escaping from Damascus in a basket let down through an opening in the wall (9:25). Barnabas then brings him to the apostles.`,
      [[`Galatians 1:11-24`, `Paul’s own account of the same period.`], [`1 Timothy 1:12-16`, `“Foremost of sinners.”`]],
      [`Jesus says “why are you persecuting me,” not “my followers.” What does that say about the identity of Christ and the church?`, `Ananias raises an honest objection and obeys. What would you have done?`],
      [`rate`, `How willing are you to extend trust to a person who has harmed others and now claims to have changed?`, `Reluctant`, `Open`]],
    [11, `Peter explains, and Antioch begins`,
      `In Acts 11 Peter is criticised by believers in Jerusalem for eating with uncircumcised Gentiles, and he narrates the events of chapter 10 step by step: the vision of the sheet, the three men sent by Cornelius, the Spirit falling on the Gentiles as it had on the apostles at the beginning. “Who was I that I could stand in God’s way?” (11:17). The critics fall silent and glorify God: “Then to the Gentiles also God has granted repentance that leads to life.” Meanwhile, scattered believers preach to Greeks in Antioch, and “a great number who believed turned to the Lord” (11:21). Barnabas, sent from Jerusalem, rejoices, and fetches Saul from Tarsus. “In Antioch the disciples were first called Christians” (11:26). The church there sends relief to Judea during a famine under Claudius (11:27-30).`,
      [[`Acts 10:9-48`, `Peter and Cornelius.`], [`Galatians 2:11-14`, `A later conflict in Antioch.`]],
      [`Peter defends his action by telling what happened, not by arguing doctrine. How persuasive is testimony in a dispute?`, `The name Christian first appears in a mixed, multi-ethnic city. What does that suggest about identity?`],
      [`choose`, `What drives the church’s decision about Gentiles?`, `The evidence of the Spirit’s work.`, `A deliberate reading of scripture.`]],
    [12, `Prison doors, and a king who is struck down`,
      `Acts 12 begins with Herod Agrippa I killing James the brother of John with the sword, and arresting Peter during Passover (12:1-3). “So Peter was kept in prison, but earnest prayer for him was made to God by the church” (12:5). The night before his trial, Peter sleeps between two soldiers, chained. An angel wakes him, the chains fall off, and he walks past guards and through an iron gate that opens by itself. At the house of Mary, mother of John Mark, the believers are praying, and when the servant girl Rhoda announces Peter at the door, they say, “You are out of your mind” (12:15). Herod later accepts the people’s acclaim as a god and is struck down by an angel, “eaten by worms” (12:23); Josephus also describes his sudden death in Caesarea in 44 CE. “But the word of God increased and multiplied” (12:24).`,
      [[`Psalm 34:7`, `“The angel of the LORD encamps around those who fear him.”`], [`Daniel 3:24-28`, `Deliverance from the furnace.`]],
      [`The church prays earnestly and then does not believe the answer. What does that say about prayer and expectation?`, `James is killed and Peter is freed. How do you hold unequal outcomes?`],
      [`choose`, `What does the contrast between James and Peter teach?`, `That God’s ways are not uniformly predictable, and faith holds in both outcomes.`, `That the church’s growth does not depend on any single leader.`]],
    [13, `Sent from Antioch, and a sermon at Pisidian Antioch`,
      `Acts 13 starts with a church of five prophets and teachers in Antioch, including Barnabas, Simeon called Niger, Lucius of Cyrene, Manaen, and Saul. While they worship and fast, the Holy Spirit says, “Set apart for me Barnabas and Saul for the work to which I have called them” (13:2). They sail to Cyprus, where Saul, now called Paul, confronts the sorcerer Elymas before the proconsul Sergius Paulus. In Pisidian Antioch, Paul gives the first recorded sermon of his: he retells Israel’s story from Egypt to David, then proclaims Jesus as the promised Saviour, citing Psalm 2:7, Isaiah 55:3 and Psalm 16:10 (13:16-37). “Through this man forgiveness of sins is proclaimed to you, and by him everyone who believes is freed from everything from which you could not be freed by the law of Moses” (13:38-39). Many respond, but jealous leaders drive them out. Paul says, “Behold, we are turning to the Gentiles” (13:46), citing Isaiah 49:6.`,
      [[`Isaiah 49:6`, `“A light for the nations.”`], [`Psalm 16:8-11`, `The psalm Paul reads as resurrection.`]],
      [`The mission begins in worship and fasting. What does that suggest about the source of direction?`, `Paul’s sermon retells history before it announces Jesus. Why start with a story?`],
      [`choose`, `How do you read “turning to the Gentiles”?`, `As a shift in strategy following rejection in a specific city.`, `As a major turning point toward the nations.`]],
    [14, `Gods in Lystra, and a stoning`,
      `At Lystra, Paul heals a man lame from birth, and the crowd cries in Lycaonian, “The gods have come down to us in the likeness of men!” (14:11). They call Barnabas Zeus and Paul Hermes (Ovid tells of Jupiter and Mercury visiting a Phrygian couple nearby, a likely background), and the priest of Zeus brings oxen and garlands. The apostles tear their garments and shout, “Men, why are you doing these things? We also are men, of like nature with you, and we bring you good news, that you should turn from these vain things to a living God, who made the heavens and the earth and the sea” (14:15). They appeal to the God who gives rain and fruitful seasons, with no appeal to scripture, since the audience is pagan. Then Jewish opponents arrive from Antioch and Iconium, and the crowd stones Paul and drags him out of the city. He gets up and goes back in. On return they strengthen the disciples: “Through many tribulations we must enter the kingdom of God” (14:22), and appoint elders in each church.`,
      [[`Acts 17:22-31`, `Paul’s Athens speech, another address to pagans.`], [`Acts 28:3-6`, `Another scene where onlookers decide Paul is a god.`]],
      [`The crowd praises and then stones within days. What does that say about the fragility of popular approval?`, `Paul’s speech avoids scripture because the audience has none. How do you adjust your language for different listeners?`],
      [`choose`, `What does Paul’s speech do?`, `It starts from creation and common experience rather than from Jewish history.`, `It rejects idolatry directly.`]],
    [16, `A woman by the river, and midnight hymns`,
      `Acts 16 starts with Timothy joining Paul and Silas. In a vision a man of Macedonia asks, “Come over to Macedonia and help us” (16:9), and here the narrative shifts to “we,” suggesting the writer joins the party. At Philippi, on the Sabbath, they speak to women gathered by a river, and Lydia, a seller of purple cloth from Thyatira, listens, “the Lord opened her heart” (16:14), and her household is baptised. Paul frees a slave girl of a spirit of divination, angering her owners, who lose income; the apostles are beaten and jailed. “About midnight Paul and Silas were praying and singing hymns to God, and the prisoners were listening to them” (16:25). An earthquake opens the doors, and the jailer, about to kill himself, asks, “What must I do to be saved?” “Believe in the Lord Jesus, and you will be saved, you and your household” (16:30-31). Next day Paul insists on a public apology because he is a Roman citizen.`,
      [[`Philippians 1:3-8`, `Paul’s fondness for the church that began here.`], [`Psalm 119:62`, `“At midnight I rise to praise you.”`]],
      [`The prisoners listen to hymns at midnight. What does your private response to hardship communicate to others?`, `Paul uses his citizenship late in the story. What does it say about when to claim rights?`],
      [`choose`, `What does the story show about the first European converts?`, `A cross-section: a merchant, a slave girl, a jailer.`, `A pattern of households responding together.`]],
    [18, `Corinth, tentmakers, and Apollos`,
      `In Acts 18 Paul goes from Athens to Corinth, where he meets Aquila and Priscilla, Jews expelled from Rome by Claudius, and works with them as a tentmaker (18:1-3). He argues in the synagogue every Sabbath. When opposition rises he says, “Your blood be on your own heads. I am innocent. From now on I will go to the Gentiles” (18:6). The Lord speaks in a night vision: “Do not be afraid, but go on speaking… for I am with you… for I have many in this city who are my people” (18:9-10). He stays eighteen months. Jews bring him before the proconsul Gallio; an inscription at Delphi mentions Gallio’s term in 51-52 CE, which helps date Paul’s ministry. Gallio dismisses the case. After a trip to Antioch, Paul returns to Ephesus, and Apollos, an eloquent Alexandrian, is taught more accurately by Priscilla and Aquila (18:26).`,
      [[`1 Corinthians 1:12`, `Divisions in Corinth around Paul, Apollos and Cephas.`], [`Romans 16:3-5`, `Prisca and Aquila greeted by Paul.`]],
      [`Priscilla and Aquila are named together, with Priscilla often first. What does this tell you about the roles of women in the early church?`, `The Delphi inscription anchors Paul’s chronology. What do you gain from a fixed date?`],
      [`choose`, `Why does the Lord speak in a vision in Corinth?`, `To encourage Paul in the face of fear.`, `To announce that Corinth’s many would believe.`]],
    [19, `Ephesus, magic books, and a riot`,
      `Acts 19 describes Paul’s time in Ephesus, where he finds twelve disciples who know only John’s baptism and receives them into the Spirit, then spends two years teaching daily in the hall of Tyrannus so that “all the residents of Asia heard the word of the Lord” (19:10). Extraordinary miracles occur, and seven sons of Sceva, itinerant exorcists, try to use Jesus’ name and are beaten by the possessed man. Many who practised magic burn their books, valued at fifty thousand pieces of silver (19:19). Demetrius, a silversmith, stirs a riot because the new faith threatens the trade in shrines of Artemis, whose temple was one of the Seven Wonders. The crowd chants for two hours, “Great is Artemis of the Ephesians!” (19:34). The town clerk calms them, noting that Paul and his companions are neither temple robbers nor blasphemers (19:37).`,
      [[`Ephesians 1:1-14`, `A letter later addressed to this church.`], [`Revelation 2:1-7`, `The message to Ephesus.`]],
      [`The riot was about money disguised as religion. How does economic interest dress itself up as piety today?`, `People burn expensive books as a sign of change. What would be your equivalent?`],
      [`choose`, `What does the town clerk’s response suggest?`, `Roman officials initially saw Christianity as a non-threat.`, `Luke is showing that Christians were not revolutionaries.`]],
    [20, `A long sermon, a fall, and a farewell`,
      `In Acts 20 Paul stays seven days in Troas and, on the first day of the week, “when we were gathered together to break bread,” talks until midnight. A young man, Eutychus, sitting in a window, falls asleep and falls from the third storey, “and was taken up dead.” Paul embraces him and says, “Do not be alarmed, for his life is in him” (20:10). Paul talks until daybreak. At Miletus, he summons the Ephesian elders and gives a farewell speech, the only one in Acts addressed to Christians: he recalls serving “with all humility and with tears and with trials” (20:19), warns that “fierce wolves will come in among you, not sparing the flock” (20:29), and cites a saying of Jesus not found in the Gospels: “It is more blessed to give than to receive” (20:35). They weep, embrace him, and accompany him to the ship.`,
      [[`1 Samuel 12:1-5`, `Samuel’s farewell address.`], [`1 Timothy 1:3-7`, `Timothy at Ephesus, facing the wolves.`]],
      [`Paul says to guard the flock from within and outside. Where do you see threats to a community?`, `A saying of Jesus is cited that is not in the Gospels. What does that tell you about early tradition?`],
      [`choose`, `What is the main aim of the Miletus speech?`, `To exhort the leaders to watchfulness after his departure.`, `To defend his own ministry and set an example.`]],
    [21, `A warning, and an arrest in the temple`,
      `Acts 21 follows Paul’s journey to Jerusalem despite prophetic warnings. At Caesarea, the prophet Agabus binds his own hands and feet with Paul’s belt and says that the Jews in Jerusalem will bind the owner of the belt and hand him over to the Gentiles. Friends beg him not to go, and Paul answers, “What are you doing, weeping and breaking my heart? For I am ready not only to be imprisoned but even to die in Jerusalem for the name of the Lord Jesus” (21:13). They say, “Let the will of the Lord be done.” James and the elders ask him to demonstrate his respect for the law by joining four men in a purification vow, and he does so. Some Asian Jews see him in the temple, accuse him of bringing Greeks into the inner court, and a mob drags him out to beat him. The Roman tribune Lysias rescues him and thinks he might be an Egyptian rebel. Paul asks permission to speak in Hebrew.`,
      [[`Acts 15:1-29`, `The Jerusalem council on Gentiles and the law.`], [`1 Corinthians 9:19-23`, `“To the Jews I became as a Jew.”`]],
      [`Friends urge him not to go, and he goes. How do you tell the difference between stubbornness and calling?`, `Paul participates in a temple vow to show respect for the law. Where is accommodation wise and where is it compromise?`],
      [`choose`, `How do you read Paul’s decision?`, `As faithful obedience to what he sensed as the Spirit’s direction.`, `As a pattern in Luke that echoes Jesus’ steady journey to Jerusalem.`]],
    [22, `Paul’s defence, and Roman citizenship`,
      `In Acts 22 Paul addresses the crowd in Hebrew (Aramaic) from the steps of the barracks. He tells of his upbringing in Tarsus, his training under Gamaliel, his persecution of the Way, and his vision on the Damascus road, with Ananias’s role. “And he said to me, ‘Go, for I will send you far away to the Gentiles’” (22:21). At the word “Gentiles,” the crowd erupts: “Away with such a fellow from the earth!” (22:22). The tribune orders him examined by flogging, and Paul asks the centurion, “Is it lawful for you to flog a man who is a Roman citizen and uncondemned?” (22:25). The tribune says, “I bought this citizenship for a large sum,” and Paul answers, “But I was born a citizen” (22:28). The next day Paul is brought before the council. The speech shows how a single word can dissolve a hearing.`,
      [[`Acts 9:1-19`, `The first account of the Damascus road.`], [`Philippians 3:4-11`, `Paul on his credentials.`]],
      [`The crowd listens until he says “Gentiles.” What word, in your own context, ends a conversation?`, `Paul tells his story three times in Acts (chapters 9, 22 and 26). What is the effect of repetition?`],
      [`choose`, `Why does Luke repeat the conversion account?`, `To stress its centrality in the story of the church.`, `To show Paul adapting his story to different audiences.`]],
    [23, `Before the council, and a plot`,
      `At the council in Acts 23, Paul says, “Brothers, I have lived my life before God in all good conscience up to this day” (23:1), and the high priest Ananias orders him struck on the mouth. Paul responds, “God is going to strike you, you whitewashed wall!” and then apologises when told it is the high priest. He shrewdly says, “I am a Pharisee… it is with respect to the hope and the resurrection of the dead that I am on trial,” and the Pharisees and Sadducees argue among themselves, since Sadducees deny resurrection and angels (23:6-8). That night, “the Lord stood by him and said, ‘Take courage, for as you have testified about me in Jerusalem, so you must testify also in Rome’” (23:11). More than forty men vow not to eat or drink until they kill him. His nephew overhears and tells the tribune, who sends Paul under heavy guard to Caesarea with a letter to the governor Felix.`,
      [[`Exodus 22:28`, `The command not to revile a ruler, cited by Paul.`], [`Acts 19:21`, `Paul’s intention to see Rome.`]],
      [`Paul splits the council by naming the resurrection. Is that skilful or manipulative?`, `The Lord’s message is, “you must testify in Rome.” How does a sense of calling steady you in confusion?`],
      [`choose`, `How do you read Paul’s outburst and apology?`, `As evidence of his humanity and honesty.`, `As a narrative device to show his respect for the law.`]],
    [24, `Felix, and a trial that drags`,
      `In Acts 24 the high priest Ananias and the lawyer Tertullus come to Caesarea to accuse Paul before the governor Felix. Tertullus flatters Felix, then charges Paul with being “a plague, one who stirs up riots among all the Jews” and a ringleader of the sect of the Nazarenes (24:5). Paul replies calmly: “I worship the God of our fathers, believing everything laid down by the Law and written in the Prophets, having a hope in God… that there will be a resurrection of both the just and the unjust” (24:14-15). Felix, who was “rather well informed about the Way,” adjourns the case. Days later he and his wife Drusilla listen as Paul discusses “justice and self-control and the coming judgement,” and Felix becomes alarmed and sends him away: “When I get an opportunity I will summon you” (24:25). He hopes for a bribe. Two years pass, and Felix leaves Paul in prison as a favour to the Jews.`,
      [[`Proverbs 29:25`, `The fear of man as a snare.`], [`Acts 26:28`, `Agrippa’s “almost persuaded.”`]],
      [`Felix is alarmed and sends Paul away until a convenient time. What does a convenient time usually mean?`, `Paul waits two years in prison. How do you endure delays that seem pointless?`],
      [`choose`, `Why does Paul’s defence stress the resurrection?`, `Because it is the heart of the dispute.`, `Because it ties him to Pharisaic Judaism and distances him from a political charge.`]],
    [25, `“I appeal to Caesar”`,
      `Festus succeeds Felix and visits Jerusalem, where the chief priests ask that Paul be brought there, planning an ambush. Festus insists they come to Caesarea. At the hearing the charges cannot be proved. Festus, wanting to do the Jews a favour, asks Paul whether he will go to Jerusalem to be tried. Paul answers, “I am standing before Caesar’s tribunal, where I ought to be tried… If I am a wrongdoer… I do not seek to escape death. But if there is nothing to their charges against me, no one can give me up to them. I appeal to Caesar” (25:10-11). Festus consults his council and says, “To Caesar you have appealed; to Caesar you shall go” (25:12). King Agrippa II and Bernice arrive for a visit, and Festus explains Paul’s case to them, admitting he lacks clear charges to write to the emperor (25:26-27). The appeal sets the route to Rome.`,
      [[`Acts 23:11`, `The promise to testify in Rome.`], [`Romans 1:13-15`, `Paul’s long desire to reach Rome.`]],
      [`Paul uses the system to escape a plot. Where do you draw a line between trusting God and using available protection?`, `Festus has no clear charge to write to the emperor. What does that say about the case?`],
      [`choose`, `How do you read the appeal?`, `As a pragmatic use of Roman law to avoid injustice and reach Rome.`, `As Luke’s way of showing God directing the narrative.`]],
    [26, `Before Agrippa`,
      `In Acts 26 Paul speaks before King Agrippa II, who knows Jewish customs. He tells again how he was a strict Pharisee and persecuted the church, then met the risen Jesus: “I was not disobedient to the heavenly vision” (26:19). He says he has said nothing but what the prophets and Moses said would come: “that the Christ must suffer and, by being the first to rise from the dead, would proclaim light both to our people and to the Gentiles” (26:22-23). Festus interrupts: “Paul, you are out of your mind; your great learning is driving you out of your mind” (26:24). Paul answers, “I am not out of my mind, most excellent Festus, but I am speaking the sober truth.” He asks Agrippa, “Do you believe the prophets?” Agrippa says, “In a short time would you persuade me to be a Christian?” (26:28). Paul replies, “Whether short or long, I would to God that not only you but also all who hear me this day might become such as I am—except for these chains” (26:29).`,
      [[`Isaiah 42:6-7`, `A light for the nations, opening the eyes of the blind.`], [`Galatians 1:15-16`, `Paul’s call in his own words.`]],
      [`Paul says, “I was not disobedient to the heavenly vision.” What vision of yours, if any, are you disobeying?`, `Agrippa’s reply can be read as sincere, ironic or dismissive. Which do you think it is?`],
      [`rewrite`, `Write three sentences of your own testimony: before, the turning point, and after.`]],
    [27, `A storm, and a ship breaks apart`,
      `Acts 27 is the longest sea narrative in ancient literature and is told largely in the first person plural. Paul sails as a prisoner with other prisoners under the centurion Julius, on an Alexandrian grain ship, in the dangerous season after the Fast (October). Paul warns against sailing; the pilot and owner prevail, and a northeaster seizes the ship (27:14). For fourteen days they do not see sun or stars. An angel tells Paul, “Do not be afraid, Paul; you must stand before Caesar. And behold, God has granted you all those who sail with you” (27:24). The sailors try to escape in the lifeboat, and Paul tells the centurion, “Unless these men stay in the ship, you cannot be saved” (27:31). Before dawn he urges all of the 276 on board to eat, giving thanks over bread. The ship runs aground and breaks apart, and everyone reaches land, some swimming, some on planks.`,
      [[`Jonah 1:4-16`, `A storm at sea and a prophet aboard.`], [`Psalm 107:23-30`, `Those who go down to the sea in ships.`]],
      [`A prisoner takes charge in a storm. What gives him authority?`, `The soldiers consider killing the prisoners to prevent escape, and the centurion stops them to save Paul. How do protectors sometimes come from unexpected places?`],
      [`choose`, `What does the shipwreck say about God’s promises?`, `A promise stands even when the means are chaotic and costly.`, `A narrative that parallels Paul’s journey with Jesus’ way through death to life.`]]
  ]
};
